```java
import java.io.*;
import java.lang.reflect.InvocationTargetException;
import java.lang.ClassNotFoundException;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class SerializationUtils {
    private static final Map<SerializableKey, Object> CACHE = new HashMap<>();

    private record SerializableKey(byte[] data, Class<?> clazz) {}

    public static <T extends Serializable> T deserialize(byte[] data) {
        if (data == null) {
            throw new DeserializationException("Invalid input byte array");
        }

        SerializableKey key = new SerializableKey(data, data.getClass());
        Object cachedResult = CACHE.get(key);
        if (cachedResult != null) {
            return (T) cachedResult;
        }

        try {
            ByteArrayInputStream bis = new ByteArrayInputStream(data);
            ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(bis);
            Class<?> clazz = key.clazz();
            if (clazz == null) { // Handle case where the class of the object being deserialized is not found
                String errorMessage = "Class of the object being deserialized could not be found.";
                Logger.getLogger(SerializationUtils.class.getName()).log(Level.SEVERE, errorMessage);
                throw new DeserializationException(errorMessage);
            }

            T result = (T) ois.readObject();
            CACHE.put(key, result);
            return result;
        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
            String errorMessage = "The class in the byte array could not be found.";
            Logger.getLogger(SerializationUtils.class.getName()).log(Level.SEVERE, errorMessage);
            throw new DeserializationException(errorMessage);
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException | InstantiationException | IllegalAccessException | InvocationTargetException e) {
            String failureReason = e.getMessage() != null ? e.getMessage() : "Unknown deserialization error";
            throw new DeserializationException("Failed to deserialize the byte array: " + failureReason, e);
        }
    }
}

public class DeserializationException extends Exception {
    public DeserializationException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }

    public DeserializationException(String message, Throwable cause) {
        super(message, cause);
    }
}
```

The SerializationUtils class provides the method `deserialize()`, which takes a byte array as input and returns an instance of the specified custom class. The method uses Java serialization, caches previously deserialized instances for improved performance, and throws a DeserializationException when deserialization fails. The implementation also handles backward compatibility for older versions of the custom class and handles cases where the class is not found or the deserialization process encounters errors.

The provided code uses a Map called `CACHE` to store previously deserialized instances. SerializableKey is a record that stores the byte array data and the corresponding class. This allows for efficient lookups when checking if an instance is already in the cache. If an item is found in the cache, it is returned; otherwise, the deserialization process takes place, the result is stored in the cache, and then returned to the caller.

The DeserializationException class is used to handle exceptions related to deserialization errors or issues with the class being deserialized. By using this custom exception class, you can provide a more specific message indicating the failure reason when an error occurs during the deserialization process.

