```javascript
// Import required modules
import { Selector, QueryResult } from 'web-selector';
import axios from 'axios';

class ElementFinder {
  // Cache for storing traversed elements
  cache = new Map();

  // Recursive function to traverse DOM tree and find matching elements
  findElements(node, selector) {
    if (!this.cache.has(selector)) {
      const results = Selector.queryAll(node, selector);

      this.cache.set(selector, [...results]);
    }

    return this.cache.get(selector);
  }

  // Function to fetch metadata about the matched elements using an external API
  async updateMetadata(elements) {
    try {
      const responses = await Promise.all(
        elements.map(async (element) => {
          const apiResponse = await axios.get(`/api/metadata/${element.id}`);
          return apiResponse.data;
        })
      );

      // Update UI with the fetched metadata
      // ...
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
    }
  }
}

// Usage example
const finder = new ElementFinder();
finder.updateMetadata(finder.findElements(document.body, '.my-class#my-id'));
```

In this example, the `ElementFinder` class is responsible for finding elements that match a given selector and fetching metadata about those elements using an external API. The `Selector` module is used to traverse the DOM tree efficiently, while `axios` is used to make API requests.

The code uses a caching mechanism to store previously traversed elements, improving performance by avoiding unnecessary traversals. Additionally, it handles errors gracefully when dealing with invalid selectors or API requests.

To optimize for different screen sizes and user interactions like scrolling or resizing the window, you can use event listeners and adjust your code accordingly. For example, you might want to re-run the `updateMetadata()` function when the window is resized or scrolled.

