2. For the Java-based microservices architecture, I'll provide a high-level design:

- **Inventory Management Microservice (IMMS)** - The core service responsible for managing inventory data. It will handle incoming requests to update inventory and communicate with external suppliers and customer databases.

- **API Gateway** - A service that acts as the entry point to the system, handling requests from various clients and routing them to the appropriate microservices based on the request URL.

- **Supplier Adapter Microservices (SAMS)** - Lightweight services responsible for integrating with external suppliers' APIs. They will handle authentication, data transformation, and caching to ensure efficient communication.

- **Customer Database Adapters (CDAs)** - Similar to SAMS, but designed for integration with customer databases.

- **Inventory Data Service (IDS)** - A service responsible for storing, retrieving, and updating inventory data in a central database. It will implement caching strategies, asynchronous processing, optimistic locking, and distributed transactions to maintain efficiency and consistency.

- **Event Bus** - A mechanism for distributing events throughout the system, allowing components to react to changes in real time. This can be used to notify other microservices when an inventory update occurs, trigger data retrieval, or perform any other necessary actions.

- **Plugin System** - A way to add new functionality to the system without modifying existing code. Plugins can be added for custom data sources, additional APIs, custom logic, etc.

- **Resource Manager** - A service responsible for managing resources across all microservices to prevent overloading and ensure optimal performance. This can include load balancing, resource pooling, and rate limiting.

- **Security Layer** - A set of services and mechanisms responsible for securing access to external APIs and databases, as well as handling user authentication and authorization within the system.

- **Monitoring and Logging** - Services and tools for monitoring the health and performance of the microservices, logging detailed information for debugging and auditing purposes.

- **Recovery Mechanisms** - Strategies for handling partial failures gracefully to ensure idempotency and recovery from temporary issues. This can include retries, circuit breakers, and bulkheads.

