Here's an outline of a modular solution using Dependency Injection and Promises for asynchronous execution, caching, and error handling. I'll provide you with the basic structure, and you can further expand it to integrate the external API, progress indicators, user feedback, and resource management.

```javascript
// FileLoader.js
class FileLoader {
  constructor(cache) {
    this.cache = cache || new Map();
  }

  load(file) {
    if (this.cache.has(file)) return Promise.resolve(this.cache.get(file));

    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const script = document.createElement('script');
      script.src = file;
      script.onload = () => resolve(script);
      script.onerror = (err) => reject(err);

      document.head.appendChild(script);
    });
  }
}

// DependencyInjector.js
class DependencyInjector {
  constructor(dependencies, fileLoader) {
    this.fileLoader = fileLoader;
    this.dependencies = dependencies || new Map();
  }

  loadAndExecute(files) {
    const promises = files.map(async (file) => {
      try {
        const script = await this.fileLoader.load(file);
        if (!script.readyState.loaded) throw new Error(`Script ${file} failed to load.`);
        if (this.dependencies.has(file)) {
          const dependencies = this.dependencies.get(file);
          for (const dep of dependencies) {
            if (!await this.loadAndExecute(dep)) return; // If any dependency fails, abort the execution
          }
        }
        script.execute();
      } catch (err) {
        console.error(`Error while loading and executing ${file}:`, err);
      }
    });

    return Promise.all(promises);
  }
}
```

In the provided code, `FileLoader` is responsible for caching loaded files. `DependencyInjector` loads and executes a list of files while managing their dependencies. You can further expand these classes by adding features like error notifications, progress indicators, API integration, and resource management.

